        System.out.println("Auth token created: " + authToken);
    }

    @BeforeMethod
    ... existing ...
}
```

Hmm, printing the token — it's a test credential for a sandbox, fine, but better print masked. Keep it simple: print "Auth token created successfully". Avoid leaking full token in logs (it's a public sandbox, low risk, but good practice).

Question: @BeforeSuite vs @BeforeClass. "один раз для дальнейшего использования" — once per suite is the truest interpretation. TestNG runs @BeforeSuite on BaseTest since it's in the hierarchy. With suite parallel="none" default — fine.

One nuance: if someone runs a single test method via -Dtest, suite still triggers @BeforeSuite. Good.

Also keep System.out.println style consistent with existing BaseTest (it uses System.out). slf4j-nop silences slf4j anyway. Keep System.out.

Note about the background mvn test-compile — still no event. It's been the whole turn... The background event will arrive when done. I'm about to run compile again — but should wait? I'll write files now, and for verification run mvn test-compile again — but the previous one may still hold the local repo lock? Maven local repo concurrent access can conflict, but reads are fine. Two concurrent mvn compiles in the same project dir could clash on target/. Safer: check the background job status first via manage_background_run with stop=false to get a snapshot (allowed when I explicitly need an intermediate snapshot — I do, to avoid clashing). Actually the guidance: don't poll just to check; call only after event or when explicitly needing a snapshot. I have a concrete need: deciding whether to launch another build that would conflict. A snapshot is justified. Or simpler: stop the old one (it's just a compile check I no longer need — superseded by the new compile) and run fresh. manage_background_run with stop=true: takes snapshot then stops. That's clean.

Then write files, then run mvn test-compile (foreground, may background again — fine).

Actually order: write files first, then stop old run, then compile once. Let me do: write 3 files in parallel → stop background → run compile.

Wait, one more design point — should AuthClient be static-method style or instance style? BookingClient is empty scaffold. Framework style (Rahul Shetty course-like) often uses static methods in client classes. AGENTS.md doesn't dictate. Static utility style is fine for now and simplest. OK.

Also: Map.of — Java 17, fine. Body serialization via Jackson (on classpath). Good.

Edge: `statusCode(200)` — doc says Success 200. Good.

